(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-2-chronic-vision-loss-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Chronic Vision Loss Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 2","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 45-year-old man is farsighted. Why does a convex lens correct him?","choices":["It filters ultraviolet light","It converges light onto the retina","It scatters light backwards","It creates several focal points","It magnifies the retinal image"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. His eye focuses behind the retina. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Two patients have uveal melanoma, one of the iris and one of the ciliary body. What do you expect?","choices":["The ciliary body tumour does better","The outlook is identical","Neither can be treated","Both are almost always fatal","The iris tumour carries the better outlook"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct, roughly 4 to 10 percent mortality against far worse. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man had cataract surgery three years ago and reports gradual clouding again. What treats this?","choices":["Laser to the clouded posterior capsule","Repeat lens extraction","Anti-VEGF injection","Pressure-lowering drops","New spectacles alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Residual epithelial cells opacify it. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman has two years of gradual blurring, glare at night and a dimmed red reflex. Which diagnosis fits?","choices":["Cataract","Macular degeneration","Open-angle glaucoma","Optic neuritis","Retinal detachment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Slow blurring, glare and a dulled reflex. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A father asks why his son must wear a patch over his good eye. What do you explain?","choices":["It makes the weaker eye work","It rests the strained good eye","It corrects the misalignment mechanically","It prevents infection spreading","It reduces intraocular pressure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The stronger eye otherwise does everything. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has a suspected uveal melanoma. What is fine needle aspiration used for here?","choices":["To deliver chemotherapy","To relieve raised pressure","Prognostic molecular testing","To confirm the diagnosis before treatment","To drain the lesion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, rather than to make the diagnosis. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with diabetes has never had an eye examination. What do you arrange?","choices":["Annual ophthalmology review","Optometry for a refraction","Review only if vision changes","Tonometry alone every five years","No follow-up while she sees well"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A medical eye risk needs the medical specialist. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 5-year-old girl has a congenital ptosis that has covered her pupil since birth. What is the visual risk?","choices":["Glaucoma","Retinoblastoma","Macular degeneration","Deprivation amblyopia","Cataract formation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An obstructed view stops the eye developing. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man has a flat, 2 mm, avascular pigmented iris spot noticed at a routine visit. What do you do?","choices":["Start surveillance imaging of the liver","Excise it in clinic","Refer to ophthalmology to document and monitor","Reassure with no referral","Refer for urgent oncology assessment"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Likely benign, but a baseline is needed. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"An 86-year-old man has a dense scar at the macula from previous bleeding. Which form of macular degeneration is this?","choices":["Neither; scarring is unrelated","Dry, because it is now stable","Wet only if it is still bleeding","Wet, because it began with neovascular bleeding","Dry, because scarring is atrophic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The scar is the end stage of the wet process. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man has painless loss of peripheral vision over years, cupped discs and a raised pressure. Which diagnosis fits?","choices":["Optic neuritis","Cataract","Primary open-angle glaucoma","Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Macular degeneration"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with central acuity spared until late. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with early dry macular degeneration smokes 20 a day. What is the most useful advice?","choices":["Stop smoking","Take beta carotene at high dose","Wear sunglasses indoors","Reduce dietary salt","Stop reading in the evening"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is the risk factor he can change. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has an iris lesion and asks how it will be told from a cancer. What distinguishes them?","choices":["Whether it is painful","Whether it affects vision","Growth, size and vascularity","Colour alone","Which eye it is in"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A nevus stays flat, small and avascular. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man asks why he lost side vision without noticing. Why does open-angle glaucoma go unnoticed?","choices":["It only affects one eye at a time","It progresses within days","It causes halos that mask the loss","It is always preceded by injury","It is painless and spares central vision early"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct, which is why screening exists. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man has a cataract but manages daily life without difficulty. What do you advise?","choices":["Repeat glasses every three months","Defer until it affects daily life","Operate now to prevent worsening","Start drops to slow it","Refer urgently to ophthalmology"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, unless another eye disease forces the issue. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman develops horizontal double vision that resolves when either eye is covered. What is the next step?","choices":["Start pressure-lowering drops","Assess ocular alignment and cranial nerves","Prescribe reading glasses","Reassure and review in six months","Patch one eye permanently"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Binocular diplopia means misalignment until proven otherwise. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman has a cataract and uveitis in the same eye. How urgent is referral?","choices":["Routine, with review in a year","Urgent, given the uveitis","Routine, as cataract is never urgent","No referral is needed","Urgent only if pressure is raised"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An isolated cataract is a routine referral. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"You suspect retinoblastoma in a 2-year-old and the offered appointment is in two months. What do you do?","choices":["Call the specialist and get it brought forward","Accept the appointment offered","Refer to a different specialty instead","Wait and reassess in a month","Arrange imaging and review the results first"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and document it if they will not. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man with a normal eye examination, normal pressure and a clear lens reports profound vision loss that does not fit any pattern. What should be considered?","choices":["Open-angle glaucoma","Macular degeneration","Optic neuritis","Functional visual loss","Early cataract"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, once organic causes have been excluded. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"An 83-year-old woman is confirmed to have wet macular degeneration. Which treatment is offered?","choices":["The AREDS2 supplement alone","Lens replacement surgery","Pressure-lowering drops","Patching the affected eye","Intravitreal anti-VEGF injection"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct, with photodynamic therapy and laser as alternatives. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman has a cataract and cannot have her retina examined because of it. Does that change the plan?","choices":["No, imaging can substitute entirely","Yes, but only if she has glaucoma","No, cataracts never interfere with this","Yes, surgery is needed to see behind it","No, wait until vision is worse"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It blocks diagnosis of other eye disease. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"An 84-year-old man with dry macular degeneration is given a way to check himself at home. Which is it?","choices":["A penlight","An Amsler grid","A near vision card","A pinhole occluder","A colour vision plate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Distortion of the grid signals change. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman with obesity describes a whooshing in her ears in time with her pulse, plus headache and brief greyouts. Which diagnosis fits?","choices":["Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis","Idiopathic intracranial hypertension","Migraine with aura","Giant cell arteritis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pulsatile tinnitus with transient visual loss is characteristic. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man has a dome-shaped pigmented mass under the retina found on routine fundoscopy, and no symptoms. Which diagnosis fits?","choices":["Macular degeneration","Choroidal melanoma","Choroidal nevus","Choroidal haemangioma","Retinoblastoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Most are found incidentally and are asymptomatic. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 19-year-old woman is told her eyeball is long. What does that predict about her vision?","choices":["No blur at all","Loss of colour vision","Distance blurred, near clear","Near blurred, distance clear","Blur at every distance"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Myopia focuses distant objects in front of the retina. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has a raised, vascular pigmented conjunctival lesion that has enlarged. Which diagnosis is most concerning?","choices":["Conjunctival nevus","Racial melanosis","Primary acquired melanosis","Pinguecula","Conjunctival melanoma"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Raised, vascular and growing. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman has new headache with papilledema and no trauma. Which test is ordered first?","choices":["Lumbar puncture","Non-contrast computed tomography","Erythrocyte sedimentation rate","Visual field testing alone","Magnetic resonance imaging of the brain"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. A mass has to be excluded before anything else. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with glaucoma asks why he must keep using drops when he sees no better. What do you say?","choices":["They will restore the lost field","They can be stopped once pressure normalises","They treat the cataract as well","They are needed only if symptoms appear","They protect the vision he still has"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Damage already done cannot be reversed. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 3-year-old boy has one eye that does not follow a toy. What is the next step?","choices":["Prescribe glasses without assessment","Patch the affected eye","Review in a year","Refer to ophthalmology for full assessment","Reassure that he will grow out of it"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Untreated misalignment leads to amblyopia. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A family asks what happens if retinoblastoma goes untreated. What is the answer?","choices":["It often regresses by itself","It becomes benign with time","It only spreads after ten years","It is almost always fatal","It causes blindness but not death"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which is why referral cannot wait. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 42"}]};
